course: biology
lesson: 106
title: The polar zone: arctic foxes, snowy owls, lemmings and reindeer
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The polar zone shows how animals beat the cold: the arctic fox has small ears, short legs and a short nose, because small body parts lose less heat, and its winter coat is so warm it hardly needs to shiver.
explain: Many Arctic animals change with the seasons: the arctic fox is white in winter and brown or grey in summer, reindeer eyes turn from golden to blue for the dark winter, and reindeer hooves harden to cut into ice.
explain: Think of your own winter clothes: a windproof jacket over a soft woolly jumper, and the musk ox wears exactly that, long outer hair against wind and snow over a soft underwool warmer than sheep's wool.
explain: Rudolph's glowing nose, Hedwig the owl and lemmings jumping off cliffs all come from films; the real stories are a nose full of warm blood, owls that hunt by daylight in summer, and a cliff scene that film makers faked.
fact: Arctic foxes moult from white in winter to brown or grey in summer so they match snow and then rocks, and their winter coat is so warm they do not start to shiver until it is about minus 70 degrees.
fact: Arctic foxes have small ears, short legs and short noses that lose little heat, while the desert fennec fox has huge ears that give off heat to keep it cool.
fact: Snowy owls hunt in daylight in the Arctic summer, when the sun never sets, and eat mostly lemmings, raising many more chicks in good lemming years; Hedwig in Harry Potter was a snowy owl, played by whiter male owls.
fact: Lemmings are small tundra rodents whose numbers rise and fall hugely every few years; in crowded years they spread out and some drown crossing rivers, but they do not jump off cliffs on purpose.
fact: The cliff-jumping lemming myth was spread by a 1958 Disney film in which the film makers pushed lemmings over a cliff.
fact: Reindeer, called caribou in North America, are the only deer in which both males and females grow antlers; females keep theirs in winter to defend food they dig from the snow, while adult males usually drop theirs in early winter.
fact: Reindeer eyes change from golden in summer to blue in winter, which helps them see in the long dark winter.
fact: Reindeer feet make a clicking sound from tendons as they walk, which may help a herd keep together in snowstorms, and their hooves are soft in summer for boggy ground and hard in winter to cut into ice.
fact: A reindeer's nose is packed with tiny blood vessels that warm the cold air it breathes, and scientists once wrote a joke-serious study on why Rudolph's nose could be red.
fact: Musk oxen have long outer hair and a soft underwool called qiviut, warmer than sheep's wool, and when wolves come they form a circle with horns out and calves in the middle.
fact: Some zoos keep Antarctic penguins such as king and gentoo penguins in chilled rooms with artificial snow and lights that copy Antarctic days and nights; Edinburgh Zoo's king penguin Sir Nils Olav is an honorary officer in the Norwegian army.
fact: Belugas are white Arctic whales that whistle and chirp, called «sea canaries»; many people now think whales should not live in tanks, and some belugas were moved to a sea sanctuary.
fact: The Arctic is a frozen ocean surrounded by land, with land predators like foxes and bears, while Antarctica is ice-covered land surrounded by ocean, with no land mammals at all, only seals that live mostly at sea.
exercise: What colour is an arctic fox in summer? | Brown or grey | Pure white all year | Bright orange like a red fox | Black with white spots
exercise: Why does an arctic fox change colour with the seasons? | To match snow in winter and rocks in summer | Because its white fur gets dirty in summer | Because the cold sun bleaches its fur white in winter | To look different to other foxes
exercise: Why does an arctic fox have small ears and a short nose? | Small body parts lose less heat | It hears and smells badly anyway | Big ears would freeze and fall off at once | Short noses are better for eating snow
exercise: Why does the desert fennec fox have huge ears? | They give off heat to keep it cool | They catch rain like cups so it can drink | They frighten away eagles | They keep sand out of its eyes
exercise: When do snowy owls hunt in the Arctic summer? | In daylight, since the sun never sets | Only at midnight in the dark | Only in winter when it is dark all day | Never, they eat stored food
exercise: What do snowy owls mostly eat? | Lemmings | Arctic fish | Summer berries | Seal pups
exercise: Do lemmings jump off cliffs on purpose? | No, that myth came from a faked film scene | Yes, when there are too many of them to feed | Yes, to swim to new islands | Only the old ones, to save food
exercise: In which kind of deer do both males and females grow antlers? | Reindeer | Red deer | Roe deer | Moose
exercise: Why do female reindeer keep their antlers through winter? | To defend food they dig from the snow | To keep their heads and ears warm in the snow | To hang icicles on for water | Because they cannot drop them at all
exercise: What happens to reindeer eyes in winter? | They turn from golden to blue | They close for the whole winter | They turn bright red from the cold | They grow twice as big as in summer
exercise: What may the clicking of reindeer feet help with? | Keeping the herd together in snowstorms | Scaring wolves away | Breaking the ice on the lake to drink | Calling the calves to feed
exercise: What is really in a reindeer's nose? | Lots of tiny blood vessels that warm the air | A small light, like a torch | A store of fat for winter | Glowing germs from the snow
exercise: What is qiviut? | The soft underwool of a musk ox | A kind of Arctic fish | The ice on a musk ox's horns | An Arctic bird's call
exercise: What do musk oxen do when wolves come? | Form a circle with horns out and calves inside | Run away in all directions | Lie down in the snow and pretend to be big rocks | Swim out into the sea
exercise: Why are some zoo penguins kept in a fridge-cold room? | They come from Antarctica and need cold air and matching light | Cold rooms stop them from flying off over the high walls of the zoo | It keeps their fish fresh for longer | Penguins cannot see in warm air
exercise: Why are belugas called «sea canaries»? | They whistle and chirp | They are bright yellow all over | They eat seeds like a canary | They fly over the waves
exercise: What is the big difference between the Arctic and Antarctica? | The Arctic is frozen ocean; Antarctica is icy land | The Arctic is land; Antarctica is only open sea with no ice | Both are the same, at two ends | The Arctic is hot and Antarctica cold
test: «Rudolph's nose literally glows, it's science» What is true? | Real reindeer noses are full of warm blood vessels, but they do not glow | Yes, reindeer noses really do glow bright red in the dark, just like a lamp | Yes, but only on Christmas Eve | No, reindeer have no noses at all
test: Santa's 9 reindeer all have antlers at Christmas. How many antlers is that? | 18 | 9 | 36 | 27
test: Adult male reindeer usually drop their antlers in early winter. What does this suggest about reindeer with antlers at Christmas? | Many of them are probably females | They are all very old males | They are all babies | They must be moose
test: In an old Disney film lemmings throw themselves off a cliff. What really happened? | The film makers pushed them; real lemmings do not do that | Lemmings really do it every year in spring when food runs out | They jump to fly to the sea | It was a cartoon, and real lemmings fly
test: «Hedwig's a girl owl, so it was a girl owl in the film, yeah?» What is true? | The film used whiter male snowy owls to play her | Yes, only female owls were used | It was a white pigeon painted to look like an owl | It was a robot, never a real owl
test: 2 arctic foxes and 3 snowy owls in the polar zone. How many legs? | 14 | 10 | 20 | 12
test: What would happen to snowy owls in a year with very few lemmings? | They would raise fewer chicks | They would raise more chicks than ever | They would turn brown to hide better | Nothing, they only eat Arctic fish
test: «arctic fox is basically a fennec fox in a white coat» What is right? | The arctic fox has small ears to save heat; the fennec has huge ears to lose it | Yes, they have the same huge ears that let heat escape | Yes, both live in the desert | No, the arctic fox has bigger ears
test: A cartoon shows penguins and an arctic fox playing on the same ice. What is wrong? | Wild penguins live in the south and arctic foxes in the north | Foxes are scared of all birds, so they would never play with them | Penguins only live in zoos | Nothing, they share the Arctic
test: «why's that penguin a soldier?» says your cousin at Edinburgh Zoo. What is the story? | King penguin Sir Nils Olav is an honorary officer of the Norwegian army | It is a real Scottish soldier dressed up in a penguin suit for the zoo parade | All Scottish penguins join the army | The penguin won a war against seals
test: What would happen if a wolf pack met a circle of musk oxen? | The wolves would face horns, with the calves safe inside | The calves would run out to fight | The musk oxen would run away in all directions and leave the calves | The musk oxen would climb trees
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the arctic fox? | Its small ears and short muzzle limit the loss of heat, and its coat changes colour with the seasons | Its large ears dissipate heat, as do those of the fennec fox | Its coat remains white throughout the year | It shivers whenever the temperature falls below freezing
exam: Identify the origin of the belief that lemmings deliberately leap from cliffs. | A film of 1958 in which the film makers themselves drove the animals over a cliff | Careful observations made by Arctic scientists | The annual migration of lemmings to the sea | An ancient Norwegian law concerning rodents
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the antlers of the reindeer. | Both sexes grow antlers, and the females retain theirs through the winter | Only the males grow antlers, which they retain throughout the whole of the year | Neither sex grows antlers in captivity | Only the young of either sex grow antlers
exam: State the principal difference between the Arctic and the Antarctic. | The Arctic is a frozen ocean surrounded by land, whereas Antarctica is ice-covered land surrounded by ocean | The Arctic is land, whereas the Antarctic is open water | Both regions are frozen oceans surrounded by land | Both regions are inhabited by land mammals such as the fox and the bear
